From f1faad50c6839330cdb46ef7787e633cacfb3b69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 22:09:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] warden: the status page never fails silently; layer
 re-applies under the firewall lock; PBR teardown is owner-aware

Field (RT-BE88U v2.7.1 running Skynet + domain_vpn_routing + Diversion +
unbound): "Warden crashed" after updating amtm and Diversion. Live state showed
a healthy 77-rule REAPER_WARDEN, no hung nvram, three rwatch incident dumps.

1. The status CGI runs stats.sh synchronously inside single-process httpd, and
   stats.sh waits up to 5 s on the counter lock that fold.sh holds exclusively
   at the head of EVERY Warden apply. Under a firewall-start storm the request
   timed out, JSON.parse threw, and the handler returned having updated
   nothing - the page sat on its static placeholders ("-" blocked, grey
   "not armed" pill, empty table), which reads as a crash. The page now shows
   an explicit "Stats unavailable - retrying" pill on timeout/error/parse
   failure (new RWDN_91, 25 packs lockstep 6769) and keeps polling; stats.sh
   waits 2 s, not 5 - every second is a second the whole GUI is frozen.
2. firewall-start (addon hooks) is forked and not waited on, and the Warden /
   Gatekeeper / rules-engine re-applies after it ran with NO firewall lock
   while rwatch's reaper_lockrun takes one - on an iptables that never uses -w
   that loses individual rules. The block now runs under the lock (the PBR
   block already did since the previous commit).
3. The PBR teardown deleted whatever ip rule sat at prefs 9001-9015 /
   9101-9115, owner-blind - an addon managing its own ip rules (this box's
   domain_vpn_routing) could lose them on every restart_firewall. Only rules
   carrying OUR fwmark form (bits 16-19, mask 0xf0000) are deleted now.

Refuted from the code, for the record: rwatch's canary only enumerates rw_*
sets (a Skynet set can never be mistaken for a Warden set); Warden's
save/restore/sweep are all ^rw_-prefixed and add-lines-only (cannot touch
Skynet-*/DVR-*); a feed update through a momentarily-dead resolver fails to
last-good and never overwrites a populated cache; hook.sh is delete-then-insert
so a Skynet position-1 fight flips once per firewall event and never loops.
